USB(4)                         P32W Unix                          USB(4)

NAME
       usb, rusb - a USB drive

SYNOPSIS
       /dev/usb    block device 2, minor 0
       /dev/rusb   character device 5, minor 0

       #include <sys/usb.h>
       ioctl(fd, USBIOSIZE, &sectors)
       ioctl(fd, USBIOREAD, &io)      struct usbio io
       ioctl(fd, USBIOWRITE, &io)

DESCRIPTION
       A USB drive (a thumb drive) in the I/O card's USB
       socket, through its CH376S controller. /dev/usb is the drive
       as a block device, in 1 KiB blocks through the buffer cache:
       give it a P32W filesystem with mkfs(8) and mount(8) it.
       /dev/rusb is its 512-byte sectors, raw, read and written at
       any offset: for copying a whole drive (up to 2 GB: a file
       offset stops there). The ioctl USBIOSIZE on /dev/rusb gives
       the drive's size in sectors (an unsigned). USBIOREAD and
       USBIOWRITE move whole sectors by number, so they reach all
       of a drive of any size: io.lba is the first sector, io.count
       how many (1 to 128) and io.buf the count * 512 bytes; for
       USBIOWRITE the descriptor must be open for writing. The FAT
       tools, dos(1), use these.

       The drive is found when one of the devices is opened: the
       first time, the kernel prints its size ("usb: a drive of N
       KiB"). Opening either device with no drive plugged in fails
       with ENXIO.

       Pulling the drive out is noticed at the next access: the
       kernel says "usb: the drive was removed", the access fails
       with EIO, and the drive's blocks are dropped from the buffer
       cache. Anything not yet written is lost, so run sync(1), or
       better umount(8) it, before pulling it out. A program
       writing to it when it goes gets EIO ("write error"); no
       access waits more than a few seconds.

EXAMPLES
       /etc/mkfs /dev/usb 30000; /etc/mount /dev/usb /mnt
              a P32W filesystem on a 32 MB stick, mounted

       od -c /dev/rusb | head
              the first sectors, raw

FILES
       /dev/usb, /dev/rusb

SEE ALSO
       dos(1), mount(8), mkfs(8), fsck(8), sync(1),
       docs/usb-design.md

BUGS
       The drive is polled: the CPU waits during a transfer, about
       150-250 KB/s (an estimate; the emulators answer at once).
       Only a drive plugged straight into the socket works (no
       hubs), with 512-byte sectors. A drive plugged in again
       after being pulled out is found by the next open. The FAT
       filesystem of a drive from another computer is not mounted:
       the FAT tools, dos(1), read and write it.
